sceneDescription:
Manuel, a lean teenage boy of about fifteen with tousled short dark brown hair, brown eyes, and a rounded jawline, strides purposefully across the cobblestone square of Dorfplatz Altdorf wearing a dark grey wool jerkin over a white linen shirt, grey-brown wool trousers, a heavy leather belt with a buckle, and knee-high leather boots—his wooden crossbow with dark oak wood, taut yew-wood bow, and iron fittings slung across his shoulder on a brown leather strap—passes directly by the tall wooden pole planted in the center of the square without lifting his gaze, his face set with quiet defiance. Behind him and slightly to the right, Lukas, a slim grade-school boy noticeably shorter than Manuel with straight short brown hair parted on the right, brown eyes, and rounded child features, wears a short brown wool tunic with a leather belt, beige linen trousers, and brown leather buckle shoes topped with a dark green wool hood; he runs half a step behind Manuel and turns his head toward the pole, his eyes tracking the tall pole where Gessler's hat hangs—a high black wool hat with a red feather fixed three meters up on the sturdy wooden shaft driven deep into the cobblestones. Around them the Dorfplatz teems with a busy crowd dressed in medieval woolen clothes, the grey overcast morning sky pressing down overhead, distant snow-capped Alpine peaks barely visible through the cloud cover at the edges of the square. The cobblestones stretch open and uncluttered in the foreground, the stone and half-timbered buildings of the village rising along the square's perimeter. A wide shot captures the tension between Manuel's brazen passage and Lukas's hesitant backward glance, the moment suspended before consequence.

